  "question": {
    "text": "The figure below is the plot of potential energy versus internuclear distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>)</mo></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> molecule in the electronic ground state. What is the value of the net potential energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> (as indicated in the figure) in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>kJ</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> at which the electron-electron repulsion and the nucleus-nucleus repulsion energies are absent? As reference, the potential energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> atom is taken as zero when its electron and the nucleus are infinitely far apart.\r\n\r\nUse Avogadro constant as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.023</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>23</mn></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"assets\\qt0004_161161811434.png\"/>\r\nMark total potential energy for 2 H-atom (per mole) as answer."
  },
